  "tutorialChooseTitle": "Welcome to Foss Lift",
  "@tutorialChooseTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the card the app opens on the first time it is launched, which asks which of two tours to take. Foss Lift is the app's name and stays as it is. One line on a card about 320 logical pixels wide."
  },
  "tutorialChooseBody": "Choose a tutorial option, or find them later in Profile.",
  "@tutorialChooseBody": {
    "description": "Body of the first-run card, above the two tour buttons. It must not rank the two — somebody who knows their way around a gym is not taking the lesser option. Two lines."
  },
  "tutorialTrackFullHint": "Explain everything, including gym terms",
  "@tutorialTrackFullHint": {
    "description": "The one line under the Full tour button saying what is in it. Not a recommendation — a description. One line, about 40 characters."
  },
  "tutorialTrackQuickHint": "A brief tour for an experienced gym-goer",
  "@tutorialTrackQuickHint": {
    "description": "The one line under the Quick tour button saying what is in it. One line, about 40 characters."
  },
  "tutorialTrackBuilderHint": "Make a program of your own, step by step",
  "@tutorialTrackBuilderHint": {
    "description": "The one line under the Building a routine button saying what is in it. One line, about 40 characters."
  },
  "tutorialReplayHint": "Replay it from Profile → Help & tour.",
  "@tutorialReplayHint": {
    "description": "The single line shown at the bottom of the screen when somebody skips or declines the tour, saying where to find it again. 'Profile' names a tab and 'Help & tour' names an entry on it, so both must match how those are translated. It fades away by itself — one line."
  },
  "tutorialTabTodayBody": "The main page. Shows your upcoming workouts.",
  "@tutorialTabTodayBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about the Today tab. Two lines — the callout sits above the bottom navigation bar with little room."
  },
  "tutorialRoutinesBody": "A routine is a training program — the set of workouts you rotate through, like Push, Pull and Legs. Pre-built and custom routines live here.",
  "@tutorialRoutinesBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about the Routines tab. This is where the tour defines the word 'routine' for somebody who has never trained, so keep the definition; Push, Pull and Legs are the names of the shipped demo routine's days and should match how those are translated. Three lines."
  },
  "tutorialHistoryBody": "Every session you finish is logged here: what you lifted, and when.",
  "@tutorialHistoryBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about the History tab. Two lines — the callout sits above the bottom navigation bar with little room."
  },
  "tutorialProfileBody": "Find the settings, your exercise library, and the tutorial tour here.",
  "@tutorialProfileBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about the Profile tab. Two lines — the callout sits above the bottom navigation bar with little room."
  },
  "tutorialBackToTodayBody": "Now we'll cover Today in depth.",
  "@tutorialBackToTodayBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step returning to the Today tab, which introduces the run of steps about what is on it. 'Today' names a tab. One line — the callout sits above the bottom navigation bar."
  },
  "tutorialTodayRoutineBody": "A routine is a training program: the workouts you rotate through, each one a session's worth of exercises. Get one here, and Today lists them with the one you are due next highlighted.",
  "@tutorialTodayRoutineBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about the routine on Today. A fresh install has an empty routine list, so this is where the tour defines 'routine' and 'workout' and says that this screen is where a routine comes from — keep both definitions rather than shortening to the nouns. Budget four lines."
  },
  "tutorialLifetimeBody": "Every session you finish adds to your volume (overall weight lifted), sets and reps here.",
  "@tutorialLifetimeBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about the lifetime totals card. The parenthesis glosses 'volume', which is the gym term for weight times reps summed over every set — keep the gloss, and match how todayStatVolume was translated. Two or three lines; this callout floats beside the card it points at."
  },
  "tutorialSessionOpenBody": "Open a workout, then tap Start workout to see this screen. This one is a push workout: chest, shoulders and triceps — the muscles you push with.",
  "@tutorialSessionOpenBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step explaining the jump from Today to the live workout screen. It has to name the Start workout button, because the tour has just replaced the picture and has to say why. Keep the button name matching the app's own label for it — see workoutDetailStart. The second sentence is where the tour defines 'push workout', which the drawn screen is headed with."
  },
  "tutorialBoardBody": "A rep is one repetition of a movement, and a set is a run of reps done back to back. Each row here is one set, and the outlined row is the one you are on. Tap its number to log the goal, tap again for each rep you fell short, or hold to type a number.",
  "@tutorialBoardBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about logging a set: what the two words mean, then the three gestures a set row takes. This is where the tour defines 'set' and 'rep', so keep both definitions. The card sits over a mock board, so five lines is survivable."
  },
  "tutorialWeightBody": "Tap the weight beside an exercise to change it for all unlogged sets of that exercise. Tap a set's own weight to change that set only.",
  "@tutorialWeightBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about the two weights a tap can move: the exercise's weight on its goal line, which carries every set still to come, and one set's weight, which moves nothing else. The closing phrase names one set the way the dialog does — translate it with the words you used for sessionSetOnly, and keep it distinct from sessionWarmupOnly, which is the same control on a warm-up rung. Do not explain what a set is. Budget three lines."
  },
  "tutorialRestBody": "In between sets, you rest to recover. The rest timer starts after you log a set, and you can change its duration or skip it.",
  "@tutorialRestBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about the rest timer: what rest is for, what starts it, and what you can do to it. Budget three lines, four at the outside."
  },
  "tutorialNoteTitle": "Your note on a movement",
  "@tutorialNoteTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the tour step about the per-exercise note. One line — three or four words."
  },
  "tutorialNoteBody": "Optionally, write down exercise details like a form cue or a seat position for a machine. Tap the note icon to write, and it is saved for future workouts.",
  "@tutorialNoteBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about exercise notes: what people write in them, how to open one, and that it persists across sessions. The longest body in the tour — budget four lines and do not exceed five."
  },
  "tutorialVideoBody": "Optionally, record your set to review your form later. Clip length and quality can be changed in Settings.",
  "@tutorialVideoBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about filming a set: what the camera icon does, the two ways to watch a clip back, and that nothing is uploaded. Budget four lines, five at the outside."
  },
  "tutorialShadeBody": "If you exit the app mid-workout, you can log your exercises from the notification bar. It shows the current set and the rest timer in between sets.",
  "@tutorialShadeBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about the notification. 'Done', 'Missed' and 'Skip' name the notification's own buttons — use the words you translated shadeDone, shadeMissed and sessionRestSkip with. Budget three lines."
  },
  "tutorialDoneTitle": "That is the tour",
  "@tutorialDoneTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the last step of the full tour. Three or four words on one line."
  },
  "tutorialDoneBody": "Replay it from Profile → Help & tour.",
  "@tutorialDoneBody": {
    "description": "Body of the last step of the full tour. 'Profile' names a tab and 'Help & tour' names an entry on it, so both must match how those are translated. The arrow is a separator between them and stays as it is. Three lines."
  },
  "tutorialDoneQuickBody": "Replay it from Profile → Help & tour, where the longer tour is too.",
  "@tutorialDoneQuickBody": {
    "description": "Body of the last step of the quick tour, in place of tutorialDoneBody. Same naming rules: 'Profile' is a tab, 'Help & tour' is an entry on it. Two or three lines."
  },
  "tutorialBuildNewBody": "A routine is a training program — the set of workouts you rotate through. New routine opens an empty one.",
  "@tutorialBuildNewBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step ringing New routine. This is where the tour defines the word 'routine' for somebody who has never trained, so keep the definition. It also has to say what the button opens, because the next step's picture is that screen."
  },
  "tutorialBuildNameBody": "New routine opens this. Name it whatever you will recognise on Today — “Push Pull Legs”, “Mondays and Thursdays”.",
  "@tutorialBuildNameBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step ringing the routine builder's name field. It opens by naming what got you here, because the picture has just changed with nothing on the phone having moved. The two examples are routine names a user might write; translate them as plausible names rather than word for word."
  },
  "tutorialBuildDaysBody": "A workout is one session: the exercises you do in one visit. Most programs have three to five and rotate through them.",
  "@tutorialBuildDaysBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step ringing the list of workouts. This is where the tour defines 'workout', so keep the definition. Budget three lines."
  },
  "tutorialBuildExercisesBody": "Tapping a workout opens it. Add the movements it is made of, in the order you will do them, and drag the grip on the left to reorder.",
  "@tutorialBuildExercisesBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step ringing a workout's exercise list. It opens by naming what got you here — the picture has changed to a different screen. 'Grip' is the drag handle at the left of each row."
  },
  "tutorialBuildSlotTitle": "What each exercise is set to",
  "@tutorialBuildSlotTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the builder-tour step explaining the per-exercise settings. One line, five or six words."
  },
  "tutorialBuildSlotBody": "Tap an exercise to configure it. Sets is how many times you repeat it, the rep target is what each set aims at, and rest is the pause between them. Step up by is how much is added after a clean session; back off by is how much comes off after missing one.",
  "@tutorialBuildSlotBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about one exercise's settings, shown against a drawing of the sheet those settings are on. It names five of the fields on that sheet, so use the same words you translated those field captions with — itemEditorSets, itemEditorReps, itemEditorRest, itemEditorStepUpBy and itemEditorBackOffBy. Five lines is survivable."
  },
  "tutorialBuildSupersetBody": "Ticking this joins the exercise to the one above it: a set of each, back to back, with the rest at the end of the round instead of between them. That is a superset, and it saves time.",
  "@tutorialBuildSupersetBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about the superset checkbox, shown with that checkbox ringed. It is where the tour defines 'superset' for somebody new to the gym, so keep the definition rather than assuming the word. Use the same noun as commonSuperset. Four lines."
  },
  "tutorialBuildQuickTitle": "Name it and fill it",
  "@tutorialBuildQuickTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the quick tour's one builder step, which stands in for the full tour's separate steps for naming a routine, adding its workouts and adding exercises to a day. Three or four words on one line."
  },
  "tutorialBuildQuickBody": "Name the routine, then add its workouts. Tap one to fill it with exercises.",
  "@tutorialBuildQuickBody": {
    "description": "Body of that step. The quick tour is for somebody who has used a tracker before, so it says where the controls are and does not define 'workout' — the full tour does that on a step of its own. Two lines at most."
  },
  "tutorialBuildSaveBody": "With every workout added, save the routine. It appears under Routines, and on Today once you make it the current one.",
  "@tutorialBuildSaveBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step ringing the routine builder's Save — the last step of the chapter. It says where the finished routine turns up."
  },
  "tutorialBuildDoneTitle": "That is a routine",
  "@tutorialBuildDoneTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the last step of the routine-builder tour. Three or four words."
  },
  "tutorialBuildDoneBody": "Replay this from Profile → Help & tour.",
  "@tutorialBuildDoneBody": {
    "description": "Body of the last step of the builder tour. 'Profile' names a tab and 'Help & tour' names an entry on it. The arrow is a separator and stays as it is. Two lines."
  },
  "shadeChannelDescription": "Shows the set you are on while a workout is running.",
  "@shadeChannelDescription": {
    "description": "Description of the live-workout notification channel, shown under its name in the phone's notification settings. One sentence."
  },
  "shadeFinishedBody": "Every set logged — tap to finish",
  "@shadeFinishedBody": {
    "description": "The second line of the live-workout notification once every set is logged. Tapping the notification opens the session board, where the Finish button is; nothing is written to history until it is pressed. 'Finish' is the same word as sessionFinish, the button it points at."
  },
  "restAlarmChannelDescription": "Legacy channel for rest-finished alerts. Foss Lift no longer posts to it.",
  "@restAlarmChannelDescription": {
    "description": "Description of the legacy rest-alert notification channel, shown under its name in the phone's notification settings."
  },
  "reminderChannelDescription": "Nudges on the days a routine is scheduled to be trained.",
  "@reminderChannelDescription": {
    "description": "Description of the reminder notification channel, shown under its name in the phone's notification settings. One sentence."
  },
  "reminderBody": "Training day — time to lift.",
  "@reminderBody": {
    "description": "The body of a scheduled reminder; the bold line above it is the routine's own name. Fires on a day the routine is scheduled for. One short line — a notification on a lock screen. The dash is an em dash."
  },
  "tutorialBuildSaveDayBody": "That is one workout done. Saving it puts you back on the routine, where you add the next one.",
  "@tutorialBuildSaveDayBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step ringing a workout's Save. It has to say that saving returns you to the routine, because the next step's picture is that screen again."
  },
  "tutorialBuildOpenBody": "From Today, tap Routines. Every program you have is here, and the + in the corner adds another.",
  "@tutorialBuildOpenBody": {
    "description": "Body of the first step of the routine chapter. The full tour arrives here from the Today step, so it has to bridge the two: name the tab you came from and the tab you are now looking at. 'Today' and 'Routines' name tabs — see navToday and navRoutines. The + is the button in the top right of that screen."
  },
  "tutorialBuildAddBody": "The + is where a routine comes from. It offers three: take a ready-made program, build your own, or bring in one somebody sent you.",
  "@tutorialBuildAddBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step ringing the + on the Routines tab. It has to say what the button opens, because every step after it is on the sheet the + opened. Three lines."
  },
  "tutorialBuildLibraryTitle": "Take a ready-made one",
  "@tutorialBuildLibraryTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the tour step ringing the Ready-made routines row on the drawn sheet. One line."
  },
  "tutorialBuildLibraryBody": "Ready-made routines contains published programs such as Push/Pull/Legs, StrongLifts 5×5, and the Bodyweight Routine. Open one to review its workouts, then add it to your list.",
  "@tutorialBuildLibraryBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about the routine library. It names the row — see routineLibraryTitle — so use the same words you translated that with. The two programs are names, not to be translated. Three lines."
  },
  "tutorialBuildImportTitle": "One somebody sent you",
  "@tutorialBuildImportTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the tour step ringing the Import a routine row on the drawn sheet. Three or four words."
  },
  "tutorialBuildImportBody": "Import a routine takes a program a friend shared: scan the QR on their phone, or paste the code they sent you. Nothing is added until you have read it.",
  "@tutorialBuildImportBody": {
    "description": "Body of the step about importing. It names the row — see routinesImport — so use the same words you translated that with. Three lines."
  },
  "storageEphemeralWarning": "This browser has nowhere to save your training. Anything you log will be gone when you close the tab.",
  "@storageEphemeralWarning": {
    "description": "A permanent warning shown on the Today screen of the browser build when the database could not be given real storage and is being held in memory. It must say plainly that the data will not survive, because the user would otherwise train a whole session into nothing. Two short sentences."
  },
  "storageEvictableNotice": "This browser has not promised to keep your training. It should survive, but clearing site data or running low on space would take it.",
  "@storageEvictableNotice": {
    "description": "A dismissible note shown on the Today screen of the browser build when storage is real but the browser refused to exempt it from eviction. Milder than storageEphemeralWarning: the data is probably fine. Two short sentences."
  },
  "itemEditorSchemeAmrap": "As many as possible",
  "@itemEditorSchemeAmrap": {
    "description": "Checkbox under one written-out set: the set has no upper rep count, only a minimum to beat. The \"+\" set of a 5/3/1 week."
  },
  "trainingMaxLead": "What this routine's percentages are of. One number per lift, written onto every slot that takes its percentages from it.",
  "@trainingMaxLead": {
    "description": "The one-paragraph lead at the top of the training-max screen, above the fields."
  },
  "trainingMaxMixed": "These slots differ. Type a number to make them agree.",
  "@trainingMaxMixed": {
    "description": "Shown under a training-max field whose slots do not all carry the same weight, so the field opens empty."
  },
  "trainingMaxEmpty": "Nothing in this routine trains off percentages.",
  "@trainingMaxEmpty": {
    "description": "Shown on the training-max screen for a routine with no written-out slot in it."
  },
  "seedRoutine531Classic": "5/3/1 Classic",
  "@seedRoutine531Classic": {
    "description": "Name of a shipped program in the routine library. \"5/3/1\" is the program's published name and stays as digits in every language."
  },
  "seedRoutine531BoringButBig": "5/3/1 Boring But Big",
  "@seedRoutine531BoringButBig": {
    "description": "Name of a shipped program. \"Boring But Big\" is the published name of the template; translate it only if it is known under a translated name in your language."
  },
  "seedRoutine531FirstSetLast": "5/3/1 First Set Last",
  "@seedRoutine531FirstSetLast": {
    "description": "Name of a shipped program. \"First Set Last\" is the published name of the template — five sets at the weight the session opened on."
  },
  "seedRoutine531Beginners": "5/3/1 for Beginners",
  "@seedRoutine531Beginners": {
    "description": "Name of a shipped program: the four lifts folded into three sessions a week."
  },
  "seedRoutineCanditoControl": "Candito Linear — Strength/Control",
  "@seedRoutineCanditoControl": {
    "description": "Name of a shipped program. \"Candito\" is the author's surname and stays as it is; \"Linear\" and \"Strength/Control\" are the published names of the program and its variant."
  },
  "seedRoutineTsaIntermediate2": "TSA Intermediate 2.0",
  "seedRoutineDescTsaBeginner": "The Strength Athlete’s nine-week beginner powerlifting program. Train four days per week and choose each RPE-prescribed load in the session.",
  "seedRoutineDescTsaIntermediate2": "The Strength Athlete’s nine-week Intermediate 2.0 powerlifting program. Four weekly sessions combine competition lifts, variations, and RPE-regulated work.",
  "seedRoutineCanditoHypertrophy": "Candito Linear — Strength/Hypertrophy",
  "@seedRoutineCanditoHypertrophy": {
    "description": "Name of a shipped program: the same split with more volume on its variation days."
  },
  "seedRoutineDesc531Classic": "Jim Wendler’s four-day 5/3/1 template. Each day trains one main lift from a 90% training max, followed by two assistance movements.",
  "@seedRoutineDesc531Classic": {
    "description": "The description shown on that program's preview and copied onto the routine."
  },
  "seedRoutineDesc531BoringButBig": "Jim Wendler’s four-day Boring But Big template. Main 5/3/1 work is followed by five sets of ten at 50% of the training max.",
  "@seedRoutineDesc531BoringButBig": {
    "description": "The description shown on that program's preview and copied onto the routine."
  },
  "seedRoutineDesc531FirstSetLast": "Jim Wendler’s four-day First Set Last template. Main 5/3/1 work is followed by five sets at the first work-set percentage.",
  "@seedRoutineDesc531FirstSetLast": {
    "description": "The description shown on that program's preview and copied onto the routine."
  },
  "seedRoutineDesc531Beginners": "Jim Wendler’s three-day beginner template. Each session trains two main lifts from 90% training maxes, followed by assistance.",
  "@seedRoutineDesc531Beginners": {
    "description": "The description shown on that program's preview and copied onto the routine."
  },
  "seedRoutineDescCanditoControl": "Jonnie Candito’s four-day linear program with strength and control days. The control days use paused competition-lift variations for six sets of four.",
  "@seedRoutineDescCanditoControl": {
    "description": "The description shown on that program's preview and copied onto the routine."
  },
  "seedRoutineDescSheiko2932": "Boris Sheiko’s #29–32 sequence: sixteen weeks, three days per week, ending with a max test. Percentages use current competition maxes, not 90% training maxes.",
  "@seedRoutineDescSheiko2932": {
    "description": "The description shown on that program's preview and copied onto the routine."
  },
  "seedRoutineDescCanditoHypertrophy": "Jonnie Candito’s four-day linear program with strength and hypertrophy days. The variation days begin with five sets of eight.",
  "@seedRoutineDescCanditoHypertrophy": {
    "description": "The description shown on that program's preview and copied onto the routine."
  },
  "itemEditorEffortTargetWhat": "What is an effort target?",
  "itemEditorEffortTargetExplained": "An effort target uses RPE to describe how close a set should be to failure. RPE 10 means no reps left, RPE 8 means about two reps left, and RPE 6 means four or more.",
  "rpeRepsLeft": "{count, plural, one {1 rep left} other {{count} reps left}}",
  "@rpeRepsLeft": {
    "placeholders": {
      "count": {
        "type": "int"
      }
    }
  },
  "itemEditorGzclpOnlyHint": "Choose a tier only when building a GZCLP workout. Tiers change the set and failure progression rules.",
  "itemEditorGzclStandardHint": "Use the progression method selected above",
  "itemEditorGzclT1Hint": "GZCLP’s main lift — heavy, low-rep work.",
  "itemEditorGzclT2Hint": "Secondary lift — moderate weight and reps",
  "itemEditorGzclT3Hint": "Assistance — lighter, high-rep work",
  "itemEditorProgressionTypeInfo": "About this progression type",
  "itemEditorRepsThenWeightHint": "Build reps to the top of the range, then add weight and return to the bottom.",
  "itemEditorCycleHint": "A training cycle is made up of repeating phases, each with custom sets and reps. They use a percentage of your training max, which progresses after the final phase.",
  "itemEditorWeightProgressionExplained": "After enough successful workouts, the prescribed weight increases. Repeated misses reduce it.",
  "itemEditorRepsProgressionExplained": "After enough successful workouts, the rep target increases. Repeated misses reduce it.",
  "itemEditorTimeProgressionExplained": "After enough successful workouts, the hold target gets longer. Repeated misses shorten it.",
  "itemEditorRepsThenWeightExplained": "The rep target climbs through a range. After reaching the top, the next increase adds weight and resets the target to the bottom. This type requires a rep range and cannot be combined with training to failure.",
  "itemEditorGzclT1Explained": "GZCLP’s heavy main-lift tier. Success increases the exercise weight and keeps its current sets-and-reps stage. A miss advances to the next stage; at the final stage, Foss Lift holds it for a manual reset.",
  "itemEditorGzclT2Explained": "GZCLP’s moderate secondary-lift tier. It uses the same staged failure progression as T1 with more reps and lighter weight.",
  "itemEditorGzclT3Explained": "GZCLP’s higher-rep assistance tier. Weight increases when the final AMRAP set reaches its configured target.",
  "seedRoutineDescPpl": "A six-day barbell push/pull/legs program. Pull, push, and legs repeat each week while deadlift alternates with row and bench alternates with overhead press.",
  "seedRoutineDescFitnessBasicBeginner": "A barbell introduction trained three days per week. Alternate two three-lift workouts, add weight after successful sessions, and move to a larger program after no more than three months.",
  "seedRoutineDescBodyweightfitnessRecommended": "A full-body routine trained three days per week. It uses bodyweight progressions and requires places to hang and perform rows; edit each slot when you move to a harder progression.",
  "seedRoutineDescDumbbellStopgap": "A two-workout dumbbell routine. Alternate A and B three days per week, using adjustable dumbbells or a rack of increasing weights; plank holds are entered by time.",
  "seedRoutineDescGzclp": "Cody Lefever’s novice linear progression. Four rotating sessions pair heavy main lifts with moderate secondary lifts and higher-rep assistance work."
}
